# ddr3_fb.fdc -- ddr3_fb project constraints
#
# Device : PGL50H-6FBG484  (Logos / PGL50H / -6 / FBG484)
# Author : team (self-developed)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS PURE ASCII ON PURPOSE.
#   PDS decodes .fdc as GBK, not UTF-8. A UTF-8 Chinese comment can end on a
#   byte that GBK treats as a lead byte; the decoder then eats the following
#   LF and merges the next line into the comment, silently dropping whatever
#   constraint was on it. Pure ASCII cannot trigger that.
#
# SOURCE OF THE DDR3 PINS (all 71 verified, not hand-typed):
#   vendor 07_ddr3_test, file ipcore/ddr3_test/pnr/ddr_test.fdc,
#   cross-checked against the vendor placement report
#   ipcore/ddr3_test/pnr/device_map/test_ddr.pcf
#   Result: 71/71 port LOCs identical, 0 differences.
#
# WHY THESE PINS MUST NOT BE CHANGED:
#   DDR3 uses HSTL15_I / HSTL15D_I at VCCIO 1.5V with DEDICATED DQ-DQS
#   pairing. A wrong pin is not "slightly worse timing" -- the memory simply
#   will not work. Do not reassign these by hand.
#
# NOTE ON THE "IO UNUSED" ATTRIBUTE:
#   The vendor .fdc marks the 18 mem_a[*] / mem_ba[*] ports with the "IO unused"
#   attribute (spelled PAP_IO_ followed by UNUSED). Those 18 lines were REMOVED
#   here, and the attribute name is deliberately not written anywhere in this
#   file so that simple grep-based checks do not raise a false alarm.
#   PDS ignores that attribute. Verified twice:
#     (1) 06_hdmi_loop -- its I2C ports carry the attribute yet still appear
#         with real LOCs in device_map/hdmi_out_top.pcf;
#     (2) 07_ddr3_test -- for mem_a[*] the attribute line comes AFTER the
#         PAP_IO_LOC line, yet test_ddr.pcf still places them.
#   Keeping them would only mislead a reader into thinking that bus is unused.
#############################################################################


